/*  run_world_buf_size — `worldBufSize` actually sizes bgl.world's scratch buffers.

    The setting was parsed, validated and readable, and nothing consumed it: _bglWorld.bgl
    hard-coded `[128]` because a declared array size had to be an integer literal, and the I6
    constant the compiler emitted for it had no reader. Two things had to change. A declared size
    now accepts any compile-time integer — a `#define`d one or an integer `#beguilerSettings`
    property — so the BLR can name the setting. And the setting is read in the entry-file pre-scan,
    because the BLR is parsed before the entry file's settings block is reached; without that the
    declaration sees only the schema default, which is what "readable but dead" looked like.

    The walk stops at the buffer's capacity, so a world with more objects than that is the
    observable difference: with worldBufSize = 4 the query returns 4, not the whole tree.  */
// EXPECT_OUTPUT: 4
#beguilerSettings { target = z5; informName = "none"; worldBufSize = 4; }

object a { } object b { } object c { } object d { }
object e { } object f { } object g { } object h { }

void main(){
    int n = 0;
    for(object o in bgl.world.getAll()) n++;
    print(n);
}
